riscv64 zkVM: zero fills as the ZisK memset precompile idiom

Zeroing memory cost one `sd zero` per eight bytes: prolog initialization of must-init
frame slots (a four-store loop above 96 bytes, straight stores below), unrolled zero
block stores (`= default` of structs, InitBlock) and the looped variant for larger
blocks. libziskos' memset is the precompile idiom `csrs 0x816, <address>; addi x0,
<length>, <fill>`, which the emulator executes as two instructions whatever the length.
JitZkMemsetCsr=<bytes> (default 0 = off; bflat passes 0x18) emits a zero fill of at
least that many bytes as `[lea/addi address;] li length; csrrs x0, 0x816, address;
addi x0, length, 0`: in genZeroInitFrameUsingBlockInit for the whole must-init range,
and for zero-constant GT_STORE_BLK of the unrolled and looped kinds (LSRA reserves
the one or two temporaries; address modes with an index or an offset outside simm12
and unaligned stores in strict-alignment mode keep the stores). Non-zero fills are
unchanged.

Measured on nine mainnet blocks on top of perf-62/63: block stores alone -0.15%
executed steps, with the prolog range -1.51% (1,500,368,434 steps = -2.70% versus
perf-61, -11.93% versus master); 24- and 32-byte minimums are indistinguishable.
Outputs identical. In the traced block 3.6% of all steps were zero stores, 0.7% of
them in runs of five or more.

